claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:35:08 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> I think lab-mail is still a necessary part of this infrastructure, but
>> maybe there's something I'm overlooking. Something still needs to sync
>> mail with fastmail, and serve mail to local hosts.
>
> You are right, and I was the one overlooking something. I had
> pictured mbsync running on beastie alone, but the agent hosts need a
> server to sync with too. Making lab-mail the hub means every host
> works the same way and only permissions differ. That is better than
> what I proposed.
>
> Three things change as a result.
>
> 1. The lab-mail spec on trunk says the opposite in three places. It
>    rejects queuing MTAs and names OpenSMTPD. It makes mbsync pull-only
>    and the local maildir append-only. And it makes Dovecot read-only
>    for every login. Your design needs a two-way sync with Fastmail, a
>    server that delivers into the synced maildir, and Dovecot logins
>    with different rights: agents read-only, so they still cannot
>    delete list mail, and you read-write, so you can moderate by
>    deleting. I would revise spec.org in place, alternatives included,
>    instead of writing a second spec next to it.

Yes we are certainly changing the spec. This whole email thread is about
changing the behavior, so we're not going to be held back by the spec.

I really like the idea of agents being read-only, I hadn't thought of
that. I guess the way they get mail in is via SMTP.

Basically we're using the mail protocols the way they're intended to be
used, not as a hacky file transfer mechanism.

> 2. Mail you send to people outside the list. Today smtprelay relays it
>    to Fastmail. Under the hub, OpenSMTPD would deliver list mail
>    locally. Your outside mail still has to go out through Fastmail,
>    and there are two ways to do that:

I'm not going to use notmuch to send email to people from outside the
list. We can revisit this at some point. For the time being, I'm keeping
it simple - mail gets delivered to the list via maildir, that's it. No
other email interaction.

> 3. Agent replies go to you and the list, since every pass replies to
>    all. If OpenSMTPD writes one copy per recipient into the same
>    maildir, each reply lands twice. If it rejects your address
>    instead, git send-email fails the whole message. Two fixes:
>
>    a. Accept every recipient from agents and map them all to the one
>       list mailbox. [inference] OpenSMTPD should then write a single
>       copy, but I have not checked that yet. The spec's acceptance
>       tests would.
>    b. Agents address only the list. You read the list anyway, so you
>       lose nothing, but prompt.md's reply-to-all goes.
>
>    I recommend (a) if the test confirms one copy, and (b) if not.

Seems reasonable, run the test, report results and recommendation.
